<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchos HaRe'iyah [Blessings of Seeing]<br>v'Sha'ar Brachos Pratiyos [and Other Specific Blessings]<br>Halachos 1–3<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE</span></div>

<div class="sn">Halachah 1 — Seeing a Friend: The Sparkling of the Mind and the Resurrection of the Dead</div>
<div class="hl">Based on <span class="lr">LM II:72</span></div>

<div class="p"><b>Halachah:</b> <i>One who sees his friend after thirty days blesses <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span> [Who Has Kept Us Alive]. After twelve months, he blesses <span class="h">Mechayeh HaMaisim</span> [Who Revives the Dead].</i></div>

<div class="p">For Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> says (<span class="lr">LM II:72</span>): through seeing oneself with the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, one receives greatness. The essential greatness is lowliness, etc. Through seeing, the mind sparkles. According to the sparkling of the mind, one receives greatness. Through this, illuminations of the Torah are drawn. Through this, shame is drawn. Shame is <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. <span class="h">Teshuvah</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> — eternal life. The lowliness in every Jew is revived — this is the essential Resurrection of the Dead: the lowliness of each person will be revived. See there well.</div>

<div class="p">It emerges: through seeing oneself with the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of the Resurrection of the Dead is effected, as explained well in the above teaching. Although the essential power belongs to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> of the generation (the aspect of Moshe), whose very sight alone causes the mind to sparkle until one merits the aspect of the Resurrection — nevertheless, every single Jew has a good point within him that is the aspect of <span class="h">tzadik</span>, as Rabbainu explains elsewhere (<span class="lr">LM I:34</span>). This good point (= the aspect of <span class="h">tzadik</span>) is the aspect of Moshe — for Moshe Rabbainu is the totality of all Israel, spreading into every Jew. Every Jew has within him the aspect of <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Moshe (as also explained in that teaching and elsewhere, <span class="lr">LM II:26</span>: Moshe Rabbainu is clothed within every Jew).</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, great is the peace and love among Israel: <span class="v">"love your fellow as yourself"</span> — Rabbi Akiva said: <span class="mx">"this is a great principle of the Torah."</span> Through the love between one person and his friend, they are encompassed in each other, illuminating each other and receiving from one another. The essential love is that they see each other — as Rabbainu writes elsewhere (<span class="lr">LM I:183</span>) on our Sages' statement: <span class="mx">"the Sanhedrin sat in a semicircle so they could see each other."</span> The essential love is seeing each other — not being able to bear not seeing one's friend. When they see each other, they receive from one another and each one's mind sparkles through his friend. Each one has a good point (= the aspect of <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Moshe) relative to his friend — therefore through looking, the mind sparkles.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential vitality is through the peace and love among Israel — seeing each other. The essential vitality is from the mind and intellect: <span class="v">"wisdom gives life."</span> The essential sparkling of the mind is through seeing each other — meriting eternal life, the Resurrection of the Dead. This is: <span class="mx">"either companionship or death."</span> The essential vitality is through the love of friends — illuminating each other, the mind sparkling — the essential vitality, the Resurrection of the Dead. The essential vitality is love and peace. <span class="mx">"Either companionship or death"</span> — when there is no love and there is separation, it is impossible to receive vitality — the aspect of death: <span class="v">"for death separates."</span> Separation between one person and his fellow is the aspect of death.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"love is as strong as death"</span> — love is so fierce that one would rather die, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, than lose the love, as Rashi explains regarding the love of Hashem. Regarding love between one person and his friend as well — love is so fierce and strong that one would rather die than have the love destroyed, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>: <span class="v">"love is as strong as death"</span>; <span class="mx">"either companionship..."</span> etc. The essential vitality is from love and peace. Through love — seeing each other (the essential love), and each one having the aspect of <span class="h">tzadik</span> — through seeing, the mind sparkles, and from there comes the essential life: eternal life, the Resurrection of the Dead.</div>

<div class="p">This is why we bless <span class="h">Mechayeh HaMaisim</span> [Who Revives the Dead] upon seeing a friend after twelve months — specifically when one sees him and rejoices at seeing him, as explained in the <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span>. Friends who love each other are certainly close and adjacent at their root — they need each other, each illuminating the other. When one gazes at his friend, the mind sparkles, drawing the Resurrection of the Dead. They bless <span class="h">Mechayeh HaMaisim</span> upon seeing each other — through seeing oneself with the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the Resurrection is effected. Every Jew has the aspect of <span class="h">tzadik</span> within him. Specifically after twelve months — for the twelve tribes of Hashem correspond to the twelve months of the year, vivifying the entire year. The essential vitality: Israel (= the twelve tribes) are encompassed in each other — drawing vitality to the twelve months of the year. When one does not see his friend for twelve months, he must bless <span class="h">Mechayeh HaMaisim</span> — new vitality must now be drawn (= the Resurrection), since they have not seen each other for twelve months. At minimum, one must see his friend once a year — to be encompassed in each other and draw vitality for the whole year.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="mx">"a student who came for one day"</span> — the <span class="h">Tanna</span> taught him: <span class="v">"they seek Me day by day"</span> — one day in the year counts as a year. One must see the Rav at least once a year to receive vitality for the entire year. Friends who love each other and need to illuminate each other (being from one root) also need to see each other at least once a year. When one has not seen his friend for twelve months, he blesses <span class="h">Mechayeh HaMaisim</span> — this is literally the Resurrection of the Dead. The essential vitality is through seeing each other — drawing the Resurrection.</div>

<div class="p">This is what Yaakov said to Yosef: <span class="v">"let me die this time, after I have seen your face."</span> Yaakov is the totality of Israel — all Israel is called by Yaakov's name (Yisrael). He is the totality of all the souls of the House of Israel, as Rabbainu writes elsewhere. Yosef is the <span class="h">tzadik</span> of the generation, as is known. When all Israel sees itself with the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Yosef): <span class="v">"Yisrael said to Yosef: let me die this time, after I have seen your face."</span> Rashi explains: <span class="mx">"I thought I would die two deaths — in this world and the World to Come. Now I shall die only once."</span> Through Israel seeing itself with the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Yosef), they merit eternal life (= the Resurrection). They are then certain they will die only once — in the World to Come they will merit eternal life through the Resurrection. Through seeing the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, one merits the Resurrection — eternal life: <span class="v">"the spirit of Yaakov was revived"</span> when he received the tidings of Yosef. The essential vitality of Israel is through the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Yosef) — through seeing oneself with him, the Resurrection is effected — eternal life.</div>

<div class="sn">Halachah 2 — <i>Hatavas Chalom</i> [Amelioration of a Dream]: Targum, the Holy Tongue, and Fasting</div>
<div class="hl">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:19</span> (<i>Tefilah L'Chavakuk</i>) and <span class="lr">LM I:37</span> (<i>Dirshu</i>)</div>

<div class="p"><b>Topic:</b> <i>The <span class="h">Hatavas Chalom</span> [amelioration of a dream].</i></div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Hatavas Chalom</span> was established in the Aramaic language [<span class="h">lashon Targum</span>]. The dream comes through the slumber [<span class="h">tardaimah</span>], which is the aspect of <span class="h">Targum</span> [translation/Aramaic] — the aspect of <span class="h">nogah</span> [twilight radiance], a mixture of good and evil. Through there they ascend to nurse from the holiness (see the teaching "<span class="h">Tefilah L'Chavakuk</span>," <span class="lr">LM I:19</span>). Therefore the dream is ameliorated in Aramaic — to rectify the aspect of the dream (= the slumber), so it is encompassed in holiness. <span class="h">Targum</span> is an intermediary between good and evil, mixing from both.</div>

<div class="p">When the Holy Tongue has completeness, the dream is encompassed in good (= the Holy Tongue). This is what our Sages say: <span class="mx">"all dreams follow the mouth."</span> The dream (= the aspect of <span class="h">Targum</span>) — depending on where it is drawn, that is where it becomes encompassed. Everything follows the mouth of the interpreter: if interpreted for good, it is encompassed in good, and vice versa. This is what the dreamer and the ameliorators say: <span class="h">"chalmah tavah"</span> [a good dream] etc. — declaring that the dream is in the aspect of good, encompassed in good (= the Holy Tongue). They say this in the Aramaic language — to raise the good within the <span class="h">Targum</span> (= the dream) to the aspect of the Holy Tongue (= the good).</div>

<div class="p">One fasts over a dream because the dream comes through the imaginative faculty (= the animal faculty, the aspect of the beast). Through fasting, one exits from the aspect of beast to the aspect of man, as explained in the teaching "<span class="h">Dirshu</span>" (<span class="lr">LM I:37</span>). Also, dreams come through foods, as Rabbainu explains somewhat. Therefore one fasts — to subdue the craving for eating, rectifying the flaw of the foods.</div>

<div class="sn">Halachah 3 — <i>Birchas SheHechiyanu</i> [The Blessing of Who Has Kept Us Alive]: <i>Yom Tov</i> Reveals the Will</div>
<div class="hl">Based on <span class="lr">LM II:4</span> (<i>V'Yom HaBikurim</i>)</div>

<div class="p"><b>Topic:</b> <i>The <span class="h">Birchas SheHechiyanu</span> [Blessing of Who Has Kept Us Alive].</i></div>

<div class="p">The essential <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span> is on the <span class="h">Regalim</span> [pilgrimage festivals], as explained in the Gemara. The Sages added: even on a new fruit, as stated: <span class="mx">"I also say the blessing of <i>z'man</i> on a new pumpkin."</span> And on all good tidings and occasions of joy, we bless <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For Rav Yehudah (the teaching "<span class="h">V'Yom HaBikurim</span>," <span class="lr">LM II:4</span>) explains: through <span class="h">Yom Tov</span>, the Will [<span class="h">ratzon</span>] is revealed. This is the aspect of <span class="h">mikra kodesh</span> [a holy convocation] — <span class="h">Yom Tov</span> calls out and proclaims the Will: that everything is by His will and there is no compulsion of nature at all. Through this comes the joy of <span class="h">Yom Tov</span>. See there.</div>

<div class="p">This is the <span class="h">Birchas SheHechiyanu</span> [the Blessing of Who Has Kept Us Alive] we bless on <span class="h">Yom Tov</span> after <span class="h">Kiddush</span> — after we sanctify the <span class="h">Yom Tov</span> in the aspect of <span class="h">mikra kodesh</span>, a remembrance of the Exodus. The essential <span class="h">mikra kodesh</span> is through remembering the Exodus — when He performed awesome signs for us. To commemorate those signs of the Exodus, we make the festivals — which are the aspect of <span class="h">mikra kodesh</span>: they call out and reveal the Will through the awesome signs He performed in those times during the Exodus. See well in the above teaching and you will understand. Therefore on <span class="h">Yom Tov</span> after <span class="h">Kiddush</span> we bless: <span class="h">SheHechiyanu v'kiy'manu v'higi'anu laz'man hazeh</span> [Who has kept us alive, sustained us, and brought us to this time] — this blessing pertains to <span class="h">Yom Tov</span>, which is the revelation of the Will. Through this comes the joy of <span class="h">Yom Tov</span>.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span> [Who has kept us alive] — the essential vitality is only through the revelation of the Will. When one knows that everything is by His will and there is no nature at all — then there is vitality, then life is truly alive. As I heard from Rabbainu (his light should shine): one who has no <span class="h">emunah</span> and has turned after the wisdoms of nature — his life is no life. He has no satisfaction, going without Hashem and without providence. The world is full of suffering. One who believes in Him — that everything is by His will — then he has life: when he has good, it is certainly good; and even when, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, the opposite occurs — it is also good. Since one knows everything is from Him: <span class="mx">"everything the Merciful One does is for good."</span> There is no evil in the world at all — everything is good, since we believe it all comes from Him. But one who denies, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, attributing everything to the compulsion of nature, as if everything is governed by nature, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — his life is no life at all. The instant something happens contrary to his desire — he has no life, for he has nothing with which to console himself. Even all worldly goods are nothing when one does not believe in the ultimate good. But when one believes that everything is by His will — even when things pass over him as they do, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — he consoles himself that it will all be for good, that Hashem will have mercy and make his end good. The essential life is only through believing in His will.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore we bless on <span class="h">Yom Tov</span> — which is the revelation of the Will — <span class="h">SheHechiyanu v'kiy'manu v'higi'anu laz'man hazeh</span>: the essential vitality depends only on this time (= <span class="h">Yom Tov</span> = the revelation of the Will), for through this there is vitality, and life is truly alive.</div>

<div class="p">Likewise on every occasion of joy — a new fruit, new garments and vessels, and the like — we bless <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span>. We believe everything is only through His will. Since everything is only through His will, there is joy and vitality. Otherwise there is no joy or vitality at all — even from the good, for it is a passing good with no endurance. Only when one believes it is through His will — then there is joy that Hashem has enlarged His kindness and goodness upon us, and then life is truly alive. This is why we reveal the Will and bless <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span>: to show and reveal that the essential vitality is through the revelation of the Will. <span class="h">SheHechiyanu... laz'man hazeh</span> — a new joy has come, and we attribute everything to His will and bless Him for it. This itself is our vitality and sustenance. Now that we rejoice in the good and bless and thank Him — because we believe everything is by His will — now we have vitality. Now life is truly alive. Therefore in the blessing itself we mention this: <span class="h">SheHechiyanu... laz'man hazeh</span> — the essential vitality depends on this: that we believe everything is by His will.</div>

